<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:Spark.UI.ViewModels"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:Spark.UI.Views.Controls"
             xmlns:theming="using:Spark.UI.Theming"
             x:Class="Spark.UI.Views.Panes.InspectorPane"
             x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel"
             x:CompileBindings="True">

  <Border Classes="pane">
    <!--
      Row 4 is the port list and row 5 is the editing surface, and they are SEPARATE ROWS because
      they were not. The note box, the code editor, the group title box and the port list all sat
      on one row: the first three hide themselves and are mutually exclusive, but the port list has
      no condition and is declared last, so it was drawn over the top of the code editor. Selecting
      a code block put its ports in the pane and its source nowhere, and a person trying to write
      one could not find anywhere to type.

      A code block wants BOTH at once - the ports are how you see what your identifiers turned
      into - so the fix is two rows rather than hiding one of them.
    -->
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,*,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto">
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Classes="paneTitle" Text="PROPERTIES" />

      <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Margin="10,0,10,8" Spacing="2">
        <TextBlock Classes="primary" FontWeight="SemiBold" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Text="{Binding SelectionTitle}" />
        <TextBlock Classes="muted" FontSize="11" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Text="{Binding SelectionDescription}" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!--
        E8-T31: the lacing selector, and before it there was no way to change lacing anywhere at
        all. `Graph.SetLacing` has existed since E4-T6, the mode round-trips through the file, the
        cache keys on it and the replicator obeys it - and this pane printed `Lacing: Longest` as a
        sentence in the description, which told a user about a setting and then offered no way to
        reach it. The sentence is gone; this replaces it.

        The note underneath appears for `Auto` only. Auto is the one mode whose behaviour is not in
        its own name - it means "use whatever this node's author chose", and two nodes both on Auto
        can lace differently - so it is the one that needs a line of explanation. "Longest means
        longest" under a selector reading Longest would be noise.
      -->
      <StackPanel Grid.Row="2" Margin="10,0,10,8" Spacing="3"
                  IsVisible="{Binding CanSetLacing}">
        <Border Height="1" Margin="0,0,0,6" Background="{DynamicResource SparkBorderHairlineBrush}" />
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="92,*">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="Lacing"
                     ToolTip.Tip="How this node pairs up its inputs when more than one of them arrives as a list." />
          <ComboBox Grid.Column="1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" FontSize="11"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Lacing"
                    ItemsSource="{Binding LacingChoices}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedLacing, Mode=TwoWay}" />
        </Grid>
        <TextBlock Classes="muted" FontSize="10" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   IsVisible="{Binding LacingNote, Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}"
                   Text="{Binding LacingNote}" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!--
        E8-T35: the node's own name and header colour, asked for by the client.

        A node is an instance of something, and after twenty of them a graph is easier to read when
        the six that matter say what they are *for* rather than what they are. So the name is the
        user's, the definition's name stays as the field's watermark - which is the answer to "what
        is this really?" - and clearing the field puts it back.

        The colours are the ten category fills and nothing else. Those are the ones whose contrast
        against the header text is already measured, at rest, hovered and desaturated, so a
        recoloured node is legible by construction rather than by luck. An arbitrary picker would
        need the title to flip between light and dark by luminance, which is a different feature.
      -->
      <StackPanel Grid.Row="3" Margin="10,0,10,8" Spacing="3"
                  IsVisible="{Binding CanStyleNode}">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="92,*">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="Name"
                     ToolTip.Tip="What this node is called on the canvas. Clear it to go back to its own name." />
          <TextBox Grid.Column="1" FontSize="11" Padding="6,2"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="Node name"
                   Text="{Binding NodeTitle, Mode=TwoWay}"
                   PlaceholderText="{Binding NodeTitlePlaceholder}"
                   LostFocus="OnNodeTitleCommitted"
                   KeyDown="OnNodeTitleKeyDown" />
        </Grid>
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="92,*">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="Colour"
                     ToolTip.Tip="The header colour. Default is grey; a colour is a mark you have chosen to put on this node." />
          <ComboBox Grid.Column="1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" FontSize="11"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Node colour"
                    ItemsSource="{Binding NodeColourNames}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding NodeColour, Mode=TwoWay}">
            <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                  <Border Width="10" Height="10" CornerRadius="2" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                          Background="{Binding ., Converter={x:Static theming:NodeColourSwatchConverter.Instance}}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding .}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </StackPanel>
              </DataTemplate>
            </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ComboBox>
        </Grid>
      </StackPanel>

      <!--
        E8-T30: the inputs, as a compact spec sheet.

        THE TYPE DROPDOWN USED TO BE A FULL-WIDTH CONTROL ON A ROW OF ITS OWN, UNDER THE VALUE BOX.
        One of those is tolerable; a node with six inputs is six of them, and the pane becomes a
        column of dropdowns with the values it is actually about squeezed between them. The client
        said so. It is now a small control in the left column, in the place the static type label
        occupies on every other node's row - so declaring a type costs no extra row and no extra
        width, and the row height is the same whether the port has one or not.
      -->
      <StackPanel Grid.Row="4" Margin="10,0,10,0" Spacing="4"
                  IsVisible="{Binding HasInputs}">
        <Border Height="1" Background="{DynamicResource SparkBorderHairlineBrush}" />
        <TextBlock Classes="paneTitle" Margin="0,2,0,2" Text="INPUTS" />
      </StackPanel>

      <ItemsControl Grid.Row="5" Margin="10,0,10,10" ItemsSource="{Binding Inspector}">
        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:PortLiteralViewModel">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="92,*" Margin="0,0,0,5">
              <!-- The type is on the row, not only in the tooltip. A box labelled "radius"
                   does not tell anybody what belongs in it, and a tooltip answers a question
                   the user has to have thought to ask. -->
              <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Margin="0,0,8,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="1"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Name}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />

                <TextBlock Classes="muted" FontSize="10"
                           IsVisible="{Binding !CanDeclareType}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           Text="{Binding TypeLine}" />

                <!-- E6-T11: the type dropdown, on a code block's ports only.

                     An unwired code block port is `dynamic`, so completion offered the members of
                     `object` - Equals, GetHashCode, ToString - which is worse than offering nothing
                     because it looks like an answer. This is where a user says what the port is
                     before anything is wired to it, which is exactly when they are writing the code
                     that needs completing. -->
                <ComboBox FontSize="10" Padding="4,1" MinHeight="0"
                          HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="Port type"
                          IsVisible="{Binding CanDeclareType}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="The type this port takes. Set it to complete against something real before a wire is drawn."
                          ItemsSource="{Binding Source={x:Static vm:PortLiteralViewModel.TypeChoices}}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding DeclaredTypeName, Mode=TwoWay}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBox Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                       IsEnabled="{Binding IsEditable}"
                       Text="{Binding Text, Mode=TwoWay}"
                       GotFocus="OnLiteralFocused"
                       LostFocus="OnLiteralCommitted"
                       KeyDown="OnLiteralKeyDown" />
            </Grid>
          </DataTemplate>
        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
      </ItemsControl>

      <!-- A note's text. Edited here rather than on the canvas: the canvas is one immediate-mode
           surface that hosts no controls (ADR-0013), so a caret in it would mean writing a text
           editor to avoid writing a binding. Shown only when a note is selected, off the same
           property that holds the note - one source of truth rather than two that can disagree. -->
      <TextBox Grid.Row="6" Margin="10,0,10,10"
               IsVisible="{Binding SelectedNote, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}"
               Text="{Binding NoteText, Mode=TwoWay}"
               AcceptsReturn="True"
               TextWrapping="Wrap"
               VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
               LostFocus="OnNoteCommitted" />

      <!-- A code block's source, in a real editor: syntax highlighting and a completion list
           built from the types the wires carry (E6-T11, E6-T12, E6-T7). The text is pushed in and
           read back by the pane rather than bound, because a two-way binding onto a document the
           user is typing into buys nothing and invites a feedback loop. -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="6" Margin="10,0,10,10"
            IsVisible="{Binding SelectedCodeBlock, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">
        <controls:CodeBlockEditor Name="ScriptEditor" VerticalAlignment="Stretch" />
      </Grid>

      <!--
        E8-T71'S LESSON AGAIN, AND THIS TIME IT WAS A SCREENSHOT THAT SHOWED IT (`E8-T84`).

        These two controls used to live inside the editor's grid, which is the pane's ONE `*` row.
        A `*` row gets what the `Auto` rows leave behind, and with a code block selected and a
        watch holding a value there was nothing left - so the row was squeezed to nothing and its
        contents were painted straight over the WATCH panel below. The overlap is visible in a
        screenshot taken before this step, so it is not something this step introduced; adding a
        third control to that row is what made it impossible to keep ignoring.

        Settings are fixed-height chrome and the editor is the elastic part. In an `Auto` row of
        their own they are always laid out first and always fit, and the editor absorbs the
        squeeze - which is the right way round, because an editor two lines shorter is usable and
        a checkbox drawn over a text box is not.
      -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="7" Margin="10,0,10,10" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto"
            IsVisible="{Binding SelectedCodeBlock, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">
        <!--
          E8-T59: the code font, asked for by the client.

          THE ONE CONTROL IN THIS PANE THAT IS NOT ABOUT THE SELECTED NODE, and the tooltip says
          so. It sits under a code block's editor because that is where the client asked for it,
          and it applies to every block because a font is about the person reading rather than the
          document - which also leaves the .spark format alone, and E7-T7 promises a graph
          round-trips byte for byte.

          The list is the shipped face plus the monospaced fonts on this machine, filtered by
          MEASURING rather than by asking: Avalonia has no is-this-monospaced flag, and a
          proportional face would draw a block's source over its own port tabs, because node width
          is characters times one character's width.

          It was inside the editor's row until `E8-T84`, on the reasoning that the editor
          stretches to fill that row and a sibling on the same row is drawn on top of the text.
          True, and it moved anyway: the row it was sharing is the pane's only `*` row, so the
          whole of it got squeezed to nothing when the panels below wanted the space. See the
          comment on this grid.
        -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="92,*">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="Font"
                     ToolTip.Tip="The face code is written in, for every block in the application. Remembered between sessions." />
          <ComboBox Grid.Column="1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" FontSize="11"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Code font"
                    ItemsSource="{Binding CodeFontNames}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding CodeFontName, Mode=TwoWay}" />
        </Grid>

        <!--
          E8-T84: asked for by the client, and on by default.

          THE SECOND CONTROL HERE THAT IS NOT ABOUT THE SELECTED NODE, on the Font row's reasoning
          exactly: how somebody likes to type is a fact about the person, not about the document,
          so it applies to every block and reaches no `.spark` field - which is what leaves E7-T7's
          byte-for-byte round trip alone.

          TWO-WAY, unlike the View menu's pane toggles. Those are one-way because a checkable
          MenuItem flips its own IsChecked before the command runs; a CheckBox bound to a plain
          property has no such race, and two-way is what lets the box show a remembered `off` when
          the pane is first drawn.
        -->
        <CheckBox Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,6,0,0" FontSize="11"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="Auto-format on line break"
                  IsChecked="{Binding FormatsOnLineBreak, Mode=TwoWay}"
                  ToolTip.Tip="When you press Enter, tidy the lines above the caret - spacing, indentation and one statement per line. The line you are typing is never touched, and a block that does not parse yet is left alone. Applies to every block, and is remembered between sessions.">
          <TextBlock Text="Auto-format on line break" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </CheckBox>
      </Grid>

      <!-- A group's title, on the same principle as a note's text. -->
      <TextBox Grid.Row="6" Margin="10,0,10,10" VerticalAlignment="Top"
               IsVisible="{Binding SelectedGroup, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}"
               Text="{Binding GroupTitle, Mode=TwoWay}"
               LostFocus="OnGroupTitleCommitted" />

      <!-- The watch panel. A bubble on the canvas is a glance and is cut at sixty characters;
           this is where somebody goes to read the value. Read-only and selectable, because the
           thing people do with a watched value next is copy part of it. -->
      <StackPanel x:Name="WatchPanel" Grid.Row="8" Margin="10,0,10,10" Spacing="3"
                  IsVisible="{Binding WatchText, Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}">
        <Border Height="1" Margin="0,0,0,3" Background="{DynamicResource SparkBorderHairlineBrush}" />
        <TextBlock Classes="paneTitle" Text="WATCH" Margin="0,0,0,2" />
        <TextBlock Classes="muted" FontSize="10" Text="{Binding WatchRank}" Margin="0,0,0,4" />
        <TextBox Text="{Binding WatchText, Mode=OneWay}"
                 IsReadOnly="True"
                 AcceptsReturn="True"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 MaxHeight="220" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- E6-T16: a graph containing a code block is opened, drawn, and not run. The banner is
           where the user is told so, and where they say otherwise. Saying it in the diagnostics
           pane would read as a failure, which this is not.

           E8-T71: ITS OWN ROW. It shared row 7 with the watch panel, and the two are not mutually
           exclusive - selecting a watched node in a graph that has been opened and not run shows
           both, on top of each other. That is the same mistake the comment at the top of this file
           describes about the port list and the code editor, one row later, and the fix is the same
           one: two rows rather than a hope that only one of them appears. -->
      <Border x:Name="TrustBanner" Grid.Row="9" Margin="10,0,10,10" Padding="8" CornerRadius="3"
              IsVisible="{Binding ScriptBanner, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}"
              Background="{DynamicResource ThemeAccentBrush4}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource ThemeBorderMidBrush}"
              BorderThickness="1">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding ScriptBanner}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
          <TextBlock Classes="muted" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     Text="Opening a graph from a source you do not trust is like running an unknown program." />
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
            <Button Content="Run once" Click="OnRunOnce" />
            <Button Content="Always trust this file" Click="OnAlwaysTrust" />
          </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Grid.Row="10" Height="1" Margin="10,0,10,6"
              Background="{DynamicResource SparkBorderHairlineBrush}" />
      <StackPanel Grid.Row="11" Margin="0,0,0,4">
        <TextBlock Classes="paneTitle" Text="DIAGNOSTICS" />
        <TextBlock Classes="muted" Margin="10,0,10,10" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Text="{Binding DiagnosticsText}" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Grid>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
